  "assessment_summary": "This paper develops a clear sufficient-statistics framework to evaluate the welfare effects of nudges in imperfectly competitive markets with taxes and endogenous prices, and implements it with two incentivized online experiments on fuel-economy and sugary-drink labels. The theoretical contribution is strong and well integrated with the empirical sections. The main empirical finding—that labels reduce average demand for the more harmful good but can reduce total surplus by increasing the variance of choice distortions, especially when taxes are optimal or pass-through is low—is carefully stated as model-based and parameter contingent. Nonetheless, several identification and external-validity issues deserve attention, and some assumptions underlying the empirics are strong relative to the weight the welfare formulas place on treatment-effect heterogeneity and its covariance with bias.\n\nKey theoretical points and potential limitations:\n- The welfare formulas rely on smooth demand and a constant conduct parameter (θ), and neglect higher-order terms in the demand elasticity with respect to price and nudge intensity. These are plausible approximations but are not tested empirically.\n- The price-impact result dp/dσ = (1−ρ)E[τ] (and its multi-good analogue) hinges on the condition μ·dεD/dσ = −μ·E[τ]·dεD/dp. This is reasonable in cases (e.g., constant elasticity, homogeneous τ), yet its validity is not empirically verified for the settings studied, and nudges could plausibly shift elasticities in ways that violate this restriction.\n- The sufficient-statistics welfare expressions are for marginal consumers; the empirical implementation substitutes population-level statistics, justified by precision gains and potential generalizability as marginal sets vary. This is a useful approximation, but the sensitivity of welfare signs to these moments suggests that bias from employing population moments over marginal ones could be nontrivial in some applications.\n\nExperimental design and measurement concerns (cars):\n- Bias is defined as the difference between baseline relative WTP and a “true” relative value constructed from WTP if gas is free minus self-reported fuel costs. This choice is internally consistent with the incentives but raises measurement-error risks: (i) WTP-if-gas-free is itself elicited and could embed idiosyncratic error; (ii) miles driven and gas prices are self-reported; (iii) the assumption that depreciation is age-driven (not mileage) is convenient but contestable; (iv) the “true” value omits other ownership cost differentials (e.g., maintenance, performance), potentially conflating preference heterogeneity with bias.\n- The incentive scheme is nonstandard and cognitively demanding; although the authors mitigate this (explanations and emphasizing “answer honestly”), understanding might remain imperfect (they cite this literature). If participants do not fully grasp payoffs, elicitation noise may inflate treatment-effect variance.\n- Off-MPL responses are imputed to the median of off-MPL values and outliers are removed using a wide, four-variable top/bottom 5% screen. These steps improve precision for variance estimation but can bias estimates of V ar[τ] and Cov[γ,τ], the pivotal welfare statistics. Appendix checks (keeping all or only 1% trimming) show similar point estimates but with much larger uncertainty, underscoring fragility of the variance components to data cleaning.\n- The experimental context is hypothetical for vehicles; real purchasing constraints, financing, search, and dealership interactions are absent. This likely inflates salience and could both raise mean effects and alter heterogeneity relative to the marketplace.\n\nExperimental design and measurement concerns (drinks):\n- Bias is constructed from nutrition knowledge and a self-control proxy via an external mapping (λ, compensated elasticity) from prior work, assuming linearity and unconfoundedness. This is plausible but strong; mis-specification directly affects the level and dispersion of γ and thus Cov[γ,τ]. The externality is assumed homogeneous; in reality health externalities likely vary (e.g., by BMI, comorbidities, insurance spillovers). Both choices simplify estimation but potentially mischaracterize true distortion variance.\n- Only ~22% of participants had an MPL choice realized; labels were shown on screens but not printed on shipped products. This reduces real-world correspondence and may affect the relationship between preferences, bias proxies, and realized consumption.\n- The sample is recruited via targeted social media ads, less representative than the probability panel used for cars; the sample has higher education and lower average self-control than national averages. No weighting is used. These choices are acceptable for an illustrative experiment but limit generalizability and could affect heterogeneity patterns.\n\nEconometric specification and identification of heterogeneity:\n- Treatment-effect variance is identified from the treatment-control difference in the dispersion of baseline-to-endline WTP changes, assuming equal elicitation noise and normality of τ, γ, ϕ, and ϵ. This normality assumption is convenient but not tested. The approach also presumes independence between measurement error in γ and τ (Assumption 1); in the cars study, γ uses baseline WTP, creating potential mechanical correlation with baseline-endline change. The authors acknowledge this and provide an alternative covariance check only for drinks. The resulting Cov[γ,τ] for cars—central for the welfare signs—may be biased toward zero.\n- The multi-level mixed-effects model with random slopes is a reasonable choice for precision, but given the centrality of heterogeneity, additional nonparametric or robust alternatives (e.g., deconvolution, randomization-based dispersion tests, or IV approaches for bias proxies) would further strengthen claims.\n\nPolicy parameters and sensitivity:\n- The pass-through rate ρ and relative markup μ are calibrated from adjacent literatures (hybrid subsidies, customer rebates, soda taxes). These are thoughtful choices but imperfect analogues for the specific product pairs. Results are highly sensitive to ρ and μ; the paper appropriately provides sensitivity figures over wide ranges. Nonetheless, estimated welfare magnitudes are small; sign reversals can occur for plausible parameter shifts, underscoring the need for local calibration in applied use.\n- Psychic costs are measured in drinks and suggest large negative utility for graphic warnings; the main welfare tables set I=0, while narrative explains how including I would further worsen graphic warnings. Presenting headline welfare numbers both with and without I would clarify policy conclusions.\n\nRobustness and transparency:\n- The paper offers numerous robustness and clarity checks: label-specific results, trimming checks, “marginal consumer” subsamples, rich sensitivity plots over V ar[τ], Cov[δ,τ], ρ, μ, and E[δ]. Registration is documented; lack of a pre-analysis plan is justified by structural components; replication files are promised. These are strong transparency practices.\n\nOverall judgment: The theory is a valuable, portable contribution clarifying when average effects are irrelevant and elevating distortion variance and targeting as central statistics. The empirical demonstrations are thoughtfully designed and incentivized but inherently limited in external validity and reliant on strong distributional and measurement assumptions precisely where the welfare sign is determined (heterogeneity moments). The authors are appropriately cautious about generalizability. The work meaningfully advances how we evaluate nudges and provides a template for future applied measurement, while the quantitative welfare conclusions from these specific experiments should be interpreted cautiously and as illustrative rather than definitive.",
